      "math_academy_id": "q-184074",
      "sequence_position": 14,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=8{k}^{6}+8{k}^{3}+16,$ where $k\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8{k}^{6}+8{k}^{3}+16 \\\\  & =2({{field-2}}+8) \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=${{field-4}} is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNotice that all terms in the expression $8{k}^{6}+8{k}^{3}+16$ have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{even}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8{k}^{6}+8{k}^{3}+16 \\\\  & =2({{4{k}^{6}+4{k}^{3}+8}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=4{k}^{6}+4{k}^{3}+8$ is an integer.",
